All trained seeds map $\dt{=}1$ and $\dt{=}2$ to nearly opposite directions (cosine similarity $-0.70$ to $-0.94$), consistent across both Walker and Cheetah environments. Frozen controls remain near-orthogonal (cosine $\approx 0$) and constant across all checkpoints. The full pairwise cosine similarity matrix (Figure~\ref{fig:pairwise_heatmap}) reveals that the near-antipodal relationship between $e_{\dt{=}1}$ and $e_{\dt{=}2}$ is the dominant structure: most other $\dt$-pairs remain near-orthogonal ($|\text{cos}| < 0.2$), indicating that the RSSM selectively maximizes separation along the $\dt{=}1$ vs.\ $\dt{=}2$ axis rather than learning a globally structured embedding space.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.55\columnwidth]{figures/paper/pairwise_heatmap.png}
\caption{Pairwise cosine similarity between all $\dt$-embedding vectors (exp\_008d, seed 3, step 75K). The $e_{\dt{=}0}$--$e_{\dt{=}1}$ pair exhibits strong anti-correlation ($-0.70$); remaining pairs are near-orthogonal, confirming that the learned geometry is dominated by a single antipodal axis.}
\label{fig:pairwise_heatmap}
\end{figure}

The magnitude of the learned separation---near-antipodal rather than merely distinct---is consistent with the RSSM developing maximally differentiated internal representations for the two temporal scales, which in turn may contribute to the asymmetric reward fidelity gap reported in Table~\ref{tab:rssm_fidelity} and replicated below.

